---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { loadCatalogue, PIN } from '../lib/catalogue'

const items = loadCatalogue()
---

<Base
  title="About — Beamish"
  description="Beamish, adj. — shining brightly; radiant. First recorded in 1530. A library of light and glow effects named after a five-hundred-year-old word for radiance."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-[62ch] px-5 pt-32 pb-16 md:px-8 md:pt-44">
    <h1 class="text-[clamp(2.2rem,7vw,4rem)] leading-[0.95]">
      A five-hundred-year-old word for radiance.
    </h1>

    <div class="text-l2 mt-10 grid gap-5 font-[family-name:var(--font-display)] text-[1.05rem] leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        <strong class="text-l1">Beamish</strong>, <em>adj.</em> — shining
        brightly; radiant. It is first recorded in 1530, in John Palsgrave's
        <em>Lesclarcissement de la langue francoyse</em>, the earliest
        French–English dictionary, glossing the French <em>radieux</em> as
        “Beamysshe as the sonne is”.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three centuries later Lewis Carroll used it in <em>Jabberwocky</em> —
        “Come to my arms, my beamish boy!” — and may well have thought he had
        coined it, alongside <em>chortle</em> and <em>galumph</em>, which he
        genuinely did. He had not. He had found it.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also my surname, which is the other half of the reason a collection
        of light and glow effects is called this.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="border-line mt-16 border-b pb-3 text-[1.05rem]">How it works</h2>
    <div class="text-l2 mt-5 grid gap-4 text-[0.9rem]">
      <p>
        There is no package. Every item on this site has a <strong class="text-l1"
          >Copy prompt</strong
        > button that hands your agent the pinned source URLs, the full option surface
        with the ranges that actually look good, wiring for React, Vue and plain HTML,
        cleanup and SSR notes, and the three mistakes most likely to be made. It then
        adapts all of that to the project in front of it, which is something a package
        cannot do.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your agent cannot reach the web, the second button gives you the same
        instructions with the source pasted in. Nobody hits a dead end because
        their tool cannot fetch.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prompt URLs are pinned to a git tag — currently <code class="text-l1">{PIN}</code
        > — and never to <code>main</code>. A prompt you paste today will still fetch
        the file it was written about in a year.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="border-line mt-16 border-b pb-3 text-[1.05rem]">Designed for paper</h2>
    <div class="text-l2 mt-5 grid gap-4 text-[0.9rem]">
      <p>
        Nearly every library in this space assumes a near-black canvas, and their
        effects fall apart the moment you put them on anything else. All
        {items.length} items here are built the other way round: the default palette
        is warm paper, and each one is designed to look right on it. Light on light
        is the harder problem, and the more useful one, because most of the web is
        not black.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything is MIT. Where an effect is recognisably derived from a specific
        published demo, the credit is on its page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
